Testing overview
================

The motion logic is tested in two complementary ways.

1) Host unit tests (primary regression suite)
---------------------------------------------
Hardware-independent logic (kinematics, trajectory) is compiled natively and
checked with the Unity framework under test/host/. These run on any machine
with CMake + a C/C++ compiler - no ESP-IDF, no hardware - and are the source of
truth for the core math. They also run in CI on every push / pull request.

Run locally:

    cmake -S test/host -B build/host
    cmake --build build/host
    ctest --test-dir build/host --output-on-failure

Add a new test:
- Put it in test/host/test_<name>.cpp with its own main() (UNITY_BEGIN /
  RUN_TEST / UNITY_END), then register it in test/host/CMakeLists.txt with
  add_host_test(test_<name>). Only include hardware-independent code; keep
  ESP-IDF / FreeRTOS dependencies behind interfaces so the logic stays
  host-compilable.

Optional line-coverage report (gcovr, no percentage gate):

    cmake -S test/host -B build/host -DGANTRY_HOST_COVERAGE=ON
    cmake --build build/host --target coverage

That rebuilds with `--coverage -O0`, re-runs ctest, and writes
`build/host/coverage/index.html` plus `build/host/coverage/summary.txt`.
Install gcovr first (`pip install gcovr`) if the coverage target reports it
missing. The two-command gate above stays the CI / commit-hook default.

2) On-target selftest (prototype convenience)
---------------------------------------------
The interactive serial console exposes a "selftest" command that runs the same
kinematics / joint-limit / trajectory assertions on the target (see
src/basic_tests.cpp). It is gated by the CONFIG_GANTRY_SELFTEST Kconfig option
(default y).

How to run:
1. Build and flash with ESP-IDF (idf.py -C idf build flash monitor).
2. In the serial monitor, send: selftest
3. Expect a log line similar to: "Selftest complete: passed=<N> failed=0"

For production builds, set CONFIG_GANTRY_SELFTEST=n (idf.py menuconfig) to drop
the command and basic_tests.cpp from the firmware. The assertions still run in
the host unit tests above, so no coverage is lost.
